in roaming in the forest, exercised with thought touching Rāma, shall come here.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.719,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-14-32"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5012045","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"e27b9581f1218d98729ef0fe4a27cd66e9ba923ceea6a7efb44a0fc4900b39b5","compact":"055ce7e35f8efeacfb8603da974ef9e35d5026ec43b657ef18aa2448abb1cb35","nasal":"dd56a808d83ca75cc3a30ad11b097a2b5a7ee261c6f7cd5b50ef22957d5d897e"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-12-46","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":12,"verse":46,"english":"Or she having beautiful eyes and fond of ranging in the forest, racked with sorrow in consequence of Rāma's separation, dose always frequent this forest.* *The commentator, Rāmānuja interprets this bloka as explaining the cause of Sītā's frequenting the Asoka forest.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-14-33"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5012046","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"5b7f889e49d88900633812753fb24b5d44698f23ce44eca9b782e80cf8dc2836","compact":"2c319416ce2f8a7c9541024c78d1d85e708089e29fd3a97bfec61d6e3031275d","nasal":"2c319416ce2f8a7c9541024c78d1d85e708089e29fd3a97bfec61d6e3031275d"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-12-47","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":12,"verse":47,"english":"Ere this, the chaste and worshipful spouse of Rāma–Janaka's daughter, used to love always the birds and animals of the forest.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.846,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-14-34"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5012047","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"511944ab8138095e71838732c31b68f8a18c763527df70d8ca5745988feb45cb","compact":"6975007ff0f33f121b475d400fa5e559d998c01fe11fbb1eac09b542b773bf8c","nasal":"6975007ff0f33f121b475d400fa5e559d998c01fe11fbb1eac09b542b773bf8c"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-12-48","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":12,"verse":48,"english":"(The morning has set in) and surely the beautiful and graceful daughter of Janaka fond of performing morning ablutions, shall come to this river of pure water to perform them. \"Sandhyā literally means the point of time which connect day and night. It however, generally means the period after sunset and before the night sets. But here with reference to the speech which Hanumān began by the end of night, it means the morning.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.625,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-14-35"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5012048","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"a96013c0a94d40075f697450de7271609afd541ecc4bdadd3ffd90ac36f02c7c","compact":"2cc489b87290f0a0de2c2b0a83f4dc4a30f3390774dc94a71442138faaaddd5c","nasal":"2cc489b87290f0a0de2c2b0a83f4dc4a30f3390774dc94a71442138faaaddd5c"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-12-49","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":12,"verse":49,"english":"Truly is this beautiful forest of Asoka the worthy abode of the beloved and chaste spouse of Rāma, the lord of men.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.844,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-14-36"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5012049","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"d29993680dd1b3105be7c993bf57edde77a377ce658239bad32c61c99969cbc3","compact":"b3537657c608e8b5e950d8bcb02e7500f4647ec7e6fa97ac073b2cb43f13c7e5","nasal":"035fecbd2d63bf4b249cd51401f16bf0701f37711e7a04e21e32060e0b326609"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-12-50","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":12,"verse":50,"english":"If that one of moon-like countenance breathes forsooth shall she come to this river of cool water.\"","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.736,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-14-37"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5012050","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"9a5609084e79addaa22288dd6c918173c02c116bbf0ebd00adbabca987f86608","compact":"c5b12ab93c1ef52f8241e529024e2796e47262b16a6d5c7a082a1c500865d69f","nasal":"c5b12ab93c1ef52f8241e529024e2796e47262b16a6d5c7a082a1c500865d69f"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-12-51","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":12,"verse":51,"english":"Having arrived there and anxiously expecting the appearance of the spouse of that lord of men, the high-souled Hanumān hiding himself (on the Singsapa tree) enveloped with flowers and leaves, beheld all.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.934,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-14-38"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5012051","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"dc1a54eb4427c8ee1b3b06bd27409494ab017a82d58a4f5492cc28b8953e4c92","compact":"69eec1e369397fd4fa9c5b5e9c8a986219ea75127578d4a653515a92a91586dd","nasal":"73495e2a197ce4755864d83a2034dc63b4e90c5c1b5ad0d14c36dd6e484b8421"}}]
